WonderLlama_@hotmail.com (Igor D. WonderLlama) wrote in message news:<3ac926dc.0312181128.688a7d5c@posting.google.com>...

> [snip: blind reading of scrolls -> literacy?]

> A zen character (one who is always blind) can read scrolls.  I
> understand that a shopkeeper can help you to know enough about a
> scroll to read it while blind.

One thing you might also consider is that blessed potions of object
detection will do the same thing for you.  It's a much better
late-game strategy than lugging your inventory of "a wand" (don't want
to put "a wand" in your bag of holding) 35 levels up to the general
store.  How you interpret this action in terms of literacy is your own
call.

> A blind character with certain knowlege of a scroll can "read" it. 
> One argument would be that this does not break literacy.  The
> character can not see the words, so does not need to be able to read,
> that is, translate visual representations of words into mental ones. 
> If the character has not proven to be literate before this action,
> this action does not prove that the character is literate.

I can see the argument here, but I think Nethack already has an
inconsistent view of literacy.  How do you read the scroll labels at
all in the general case? How do you read messages on the ground or on
tombstones? However, if you have a shopkeeper tell you a scroll name
(or b!oOD) and then you read it, you are able to write whatever that
scroll is identified as with a magic marker. Thus the reading of a
scroll allows you knowledge of the writing of that scroll, implying
literacy.

Regardless of the answer here, I don't think an implementation of this
would make for a better game (like you say, fun but pointless).

